# External crates the core may reach, checked by scripts/crate-boundaries.sh.
# One `LAYER: CRATE (run|build|test): REASON` per line. LAYER is contract or
# emulation, the layers an embedder links. Application entries reserve host
# presentation crates for that layer and must remain reachable; its other
# transitive dependencies are reviewed through Cargo.lock and cargo-deny.
# KIND is how the layer reaches the crate: run (linked), build (a procedural
# macro or build script and what it uses, run on the build host) or test
# (only through a test dependency of a crate of the layer). The list is exact:
# a crate the layer reaches and the list does not name fails, and so does a
# line whose crate the layer no longer reaches. A new crate is reviewed
# against the rule of the core, and a crate that touches the host (windows,
# graphics, audio, input, network, async run times) has no acceptable reason.
# The graph is cargo metadata's resolve, so a line may name a crate the core's
# own build does not compile (see scripts/crate-boundaries.sh).
# See backlog/docs/design/core-contract.md §12.1 and §12.3.
#
# contract: none. The contract crate depends on nothing at all.

# The contract, the CPU core and error types.
emulation: unemu-core-api (run): the contract every core implements, pinned to one revision; it depends on nothing
emulation: thiserror (run): error enums (conventions.md); generated code only, no host access
emulation: x86jit-core (run): the CPU core, pinned to a revision; its host needs are listed in core-contract.md §12.7
emulation: iced-x86 (run): x86jit-core's instruction decoder; pure Rust
emulation: lazy_static (run): iced-x86's immutable tables; pure Rust

# SHA-256 of journal frames and headers and of firmware bytes.
emulation: sha2 (run): SHA-256 of journal frames and headers and of firmware bytes; pure Rust with CPU intrinsics
emulation: digest (run): sha2's hashing traits; pure Rust, no host access
emulation: block-buffer (run): sha2's block buffering; pure Rust, no host access
emulation: crypto-common (run): sha2's common traits; pure Rust, no host access
emulation: generic-array (run): sha2's fixed-size arrays; pure Rust, no host access
emulation: typenum (run): type-level numbers for generic-array; pure Rust, no host access
emulation: cfg-if (run): sha2's conditional compilation macro; pure Rust, no host access
emulation: cpufeatures (run): sha2 asks the processor once which SHA instructions it has; no file, no clock, no thread
emulation: libc (run): declarations of the host C library, reached through cpufeatures on AArch64 Linux, Android, Apple and LoongArch64 Linux targets (one capability query)

# The PC config codec: pc-config reads the machine schema and, from S4,
# reads and writes the stored PC config, both TOML (core-contract.md §14.2.1).
emulation: serde (run): the PC config codec (pc-config): the machine schema's model and the stored config's (from S4)
emulation: serde_core (run): serde's core traits, for the PC config codec
emulation: toml (run): the PC config codec (pc-config): the machine schema and the stored config (from S4) are TOML
emulation: toml_datetime (run): toml's date and time values, for the PC config codec
emulation: toml_parser (run): toml's parser, for the PC config codec
emulation: toml_writer (run): toml's writer, for the PC config codec
emulation: serde_spanned (run): toml's spanned values, which the PC config codec reads for source positions
emulation: winnow (run): toml's parser combinators, for the PC config codec
emulation: indexmap (run): in cargo metadata's resolve through toml's weak `indexmap?/std` feature; not compiled for the core
emulation: hashbrown (run): in cargo metadata's resolve through indexmap, itself there only through toml's weak `indexmap?/std`; not compiled for the core
emulation: foldhash (run): in cargo metadata's resolve through hashbrown's `default-hasher` feature, which the host-side Cranelift tree turns on and the workspace resolve unifies into toml's weak `indexmap?/std` path; not compiled for the core
emulation: equivalent (run): in cargo metadata's resolve through indexmap, itself there only through toml's weak `indexmap?/std`; not compiled for the core

# Build time: derive macros and one build script. They run on the build host
# and are not linked into the core.
emulation: thiserror-impl (build): thiserror's derive macro
emulation: serde_derive (build): serde's derive macro, for the PC config codec
emulation: proc-macro2 (build): token streams for the derive macros
emulation: quote (build): code generation for the derive macros
emulation: syn (build): Rust syntax for the derive macros; two versions, version 3 through serde_derive for the PC config codec
emulation: unicode-ident (build): identifier tables for proc-macro2
emulation: version_check (build): generic-array's build script reads the compiler version

# Only the application may link host presentation and I/O.
application: machine-view (run): shared host session, keys, pointer capture, pacing and audio
application: winit (run): the application's window and physical input events
application: softbuffer (run): the application's software picture surface
application: cpal (run): host audio output through machine-view; never linked into the core
application: unemu-test-core (test): scripted contract machine for window-free application tests
emulation: memchr (run): pure byte search through toml and winnow, enabled by host dependency feature unification in the workspace resolve
